WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.229
The Mandalorian, the first-ever live action
Star Wars TV show, arrives this November on

00:00:06.229 --> 00:00:07.390
Disney+.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:12.000
From nods to past Star Wars films to some
classic cinema heritage, here are all the

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.830
Easter eggs, references, and other details
you probably missed in The Mandalorian's debut

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.160
trailer at D23.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.550
Stormtroopers, the Empire's most common foot
soldiers, are famous for their iconic all-white

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.630
armor, but that's not the only color they
wear.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.390
Mudtroopers, who first appeared in Solo: A
Star Wars Story, wear green.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.060
Sith troopers, who are set to debut in The
Rise of Skywalker, wear red.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.020
Death troopers, who we first met in Rogue
One, dress in all black.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:46.030
According to Star Wars lore, death troopers
are the Empire's elite fighting forces.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.850
Seemingly led by a character played by Giancarlo
Esposito, the death troopers look poised to

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.160
live up to their name.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:57.520
The Star Wars universe is full of weird little
critters, and there's not nearly enough time

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.850
in the feature films to spotlight them all.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.580
Take the blurrg, for example.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.820
These oddly-shaped bipedal creatures have
never actually appeared on the big screen.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.750
Blurrgs debuted in Ewoks: The Battle for Endor,
a made-for-TV movie that aired in 1985, and

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.270
have popped up in a few episodes of The Clone
Wars and a handful of video games since.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:22.880
However, in The Mandalorian, the titular bounty
hunter faces off against a blurrg with a rider

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:23.880
on top.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.170
If you look carefully, you'll see another
blurrg, presumably dead, lying on the left

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.300
side of the screen.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.690
Also, if you were wondering, the alien riding
the blurrg is called an Ugnaught, a race that

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.560
hasn't been seen in a live-action production
since the original trilogy.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:44.950
During The Mandalorian era, Luke, Leia, Han,
and Chewie are busy establishing a new government

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.100
elsewhere in the galaxy, making any cameos
unlikely.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.990
However, some fans think they've caught sight
of one well-known character in The Mandalorian

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:53.990
trailer.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:59.439
Many viewers think the droid featured in the
Mandalorian trailer is IG-88, one of the bounty

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:03.270
hunters that Darth Vader sends after Han Solo
in The Empire Strikes Back.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.420
They look similar, but that's actually a new
character called IG-11, and he's voiced by

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.920
Taika Waititi.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.120
We'll just have to wait and see to find out
if IG-11 is as funny as the last character

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.760
Waititi lent his voice to.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.750
"Allow me to introduce myself, my name is
Korg, I’m kind of like the leader in here,

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.901
I'm made of rocks, as you can see, but don't
let that intimidate you.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.880
You don’t need to be afraid unless you're
made of scissors."

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.840
If the Mandalorian reminds you of Jango Fett
or his son, Boba, that's on purpose.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.630
Jango, Boba, and the new nameless bounty hunter
all wear Mandalorian armor.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:41.620
Made from a precious metal called beskar,
the armor can withstand heavy blaster bolts.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.300
However, the Mandalorian's outfit isn't a
complete set of Mandalorian armor.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.349
Some pieces are missing, and he's been forced
to cobble together an outfit from whatever

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:51.430
he can find.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.990
For example, look at the right shoulder pad.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.160
It's blue and grey, instead of rust red metal
like the rest of his suit.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.440
Like any good bounty hunter, the Mandalorian
is armed to the teeth.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.180
In the trailer, he wields a blaster-pistol.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.910
He has a "fibercord whip," which he uses to
snag a runaway foe.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.750
The Mandalorian's most interesting weapon,
however, is one that you might've seen before.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.110
It's the rifle slung on his back.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:20.080
Boba Fett wielded that exact same kind of
weapon in "The Faithful Wookiee," the animated

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.170
short that introduced Fett to the world.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:26.769
"The Faithful Wookiee" originally aired as
part as the infamously terrible Star Wars

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:32.300
Holiday Special, a Star Wars spin-off so atrocious
that George Lucas once said he wants to destroy

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.280
every copy.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.320
Aside from the Tatooine-like planet featured
in the trailer, we also see a few glimpses

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.590
of a lush forest world full of trees and water.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.500
In the trailer's most memorable scene, you
can see the world outside.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.580
It's not a desert.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:47.720
It's not a forest.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.900
From what we can tell, it's full of snow.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.940
So, that's three different biomes in one brief
preview.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.560
If you're tired of the same old Star Wars
locations, that's a good sign of things to

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.440
come.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.319
At the end of The Mandalorian teaser, the
bounty hunter stands in the office of an old

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:04.319
man.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:08.690
Film buffs will recognize that man as Werner
Herzog, director of countless unflinching

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.370
films like Grizzly Man and one of the most
critically acclaimed filmmakers of all time.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.900
He also acts, occasionally, like when he cameoed
on Parks and Recreation.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:23.490
"After 47 years of living here, I decided
to move to Orlando, to be closer to Disney

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:24.780
World."

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.290
In the trailer, Herzog's character seems to
be wearing a medallion engraved with a six-point

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:33.470
symbol - one that looks a lot like the insignia
of the Galactic Empire.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.199
The medal implies that Herzog is playing a
former Imperial officer, raising all kinds

00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:39.199
of concerns.

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:43.540
The Mandalorian is set a few years after the
Battle of Jakku, where the Empire made its

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:44.540
last stand.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.840
So why is Herzog's character still in charge?

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.440
And why are there so many Stormtroopers lurking
about if the Empire is dead and gone?

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.639
The Mandalorian doesn't just draw inspiration
from the Star Wars movies.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:01.630
It also owes a huge debt to spaghetti westerns,
especially those by The Good, the Bad, and

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.310
the Ugly director Sergio Leone.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:08.720
Like Clint Eastwood's Man With No Name, the
Mandalorian is a man of few words.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.920
An old fashioned saloon fight provides some
action, and the whole trailer is infused with

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.600
a sense of quiet isolation, just like Leone's
best films.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:23.120
Late in The Mandalorian trailer, a quick shot
shows an alien frozen in carbonite, much like

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.620
Han Solo was at the end of The Empire Strikes
Back.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.460
Carbonite-encased Han is one of the most iconic
images in the original series, and it's linked

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.710
with Boba Fett, who also wore Mandalorian
armor.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.240
However, if you look closer, you'll see that
there's actually four figures frozen in carbonite,

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.770
all standing in a row.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:45.840
That might be a big change from the original
Star Wars trilogy, where freezing living creatures

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.150
in carbonite is extremely dangerous.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.850
By the time that The Mandalorian takes place,
those concerns appear to be gone.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.060
People are freezing others in carbonite in
groups, and it looks like it's now standard

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.250
practice among bounty hunters.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.850
Or maybe bounty hunters are just even bigger
jerks than they were before.

